      "text": "To determine the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\), we first need to find the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\). Performing the division, we get:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{7} = 0.\\overline{142857} \\]\nThis means that the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is the repeating sequence \"142857\" repeated indefinitely. The sequence \"142857\" has a length of 6 digits.\n\nNext, we need to determine the position of the 100th digit within the repeating sequence. Since the sequence repeats every 6 digits, we can find the position of the 100th digit by calculating the remainder when 100 is divided by 6. This can be done using the modulo operation:\n\\[ 100 \\mod 6 = 4 \\]\nThis tells us that the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating sequence \"142857\". The 4th digit in \"142857\" is 8.\n\nTherefore, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
