      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\), we first need to determine the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\).\n\n1. **Finding the decimal representation of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\):**\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{7} = 0.\\overline{142857}\n\\]\n\nThis means that the decimal part is a repeating sequence \"142857\" which has a length of 6 digits.\n\n2. **Locating the repeating block and the position of digits:**\n\nThe repeating block is the sequence \"142857\", which has 6 digits. To find the specific digit (the 100th digit) within one period (6 digits) without explicitly listing all 6 digits, we divide the position 100 by 6:\n\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\n\nThis means that the remainder is 4. Digit positions in the repeating decimal 0.\\overline{142857} are as follows:\n- 1st digit: \\( 1 \\)\n- 2nd digit: \\( 4 \\)\n- 3rd digit: \\( 2 \\)\n- 4th digit: \\( 8 \\)\n- \\( \\ldots \\)\n\nIn our remainder calculation, a remainder of 4 corresponds to the 4th digit of the repeating block. \n\n3. **Extracting the 4th digit in the repeating block:**\n\nThe repeating sequence is \"142857\".\nThe 4th digit is \\( 2 \\).\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n\nThe 100th digit after the decimal point of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is 2.\n\nThus, the final answer in box notation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
